<exercisegroup>
  <title>Reducing linear systems</title>
  
  
<introduction>
  <p>
    Using augmented matrices, (a) perform <xref ref="proc_GE" text="custom">Gaussian elimination</xref> to find a row echelon form linear system equivalent to the given linear system, and (b) perform <xref ref="proc_GaussJordan" text="custom">Gauss-Jordan elimination</xref> to find a reduced row echelon form linear system equivalent to the given linear system.
  </p>
  <p>
    Make sure to bring the system into standard form before associating to it an augmented matrix. 
  </p>
</introduction>
<exercise xml:id="s_ge_equivsys">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <md>
        \begin{linsys}{4} x_1\amp +\amp 2x_2\amp =\amp x_3\amp +\amp x_4\amp +\amp 3\\ 3x_1\amp +\amp 6x_2\amp =\amp 2x_3\amp -\amp 4x_4\amp +\amp 8\\ -x_1\amp +\amp 2x_3\amp =\amp 2x_2\amp -\amp x_4\amp -\amp 1 \end{linsys}
      </md>
    </p>
  </statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    First bring the system into standard form:
    <md> L\colon \
      \begin{linsys}{4} x_1\amp +\amp 2x_2\amp -\amp x_3\amp-\amp x_4\amp=\amp 3\\ 3x_1\amp +\amp 6x_2\amp - \amp 2x_3\amp + \amp 4x_4\amp =\amp 8\\ -x_1\amp - \amp 2x_2 \amp +\amp 2x_3\amp + \amp x_4\amp = \amp -1 \end{linsys}
    </md>.
    Now perform Gaussian elimination on the associated augmented matrix:
    <md>
      <mrow>\begin{bmatrix}1\amp 2\amp -1\amp -1\amp 3\\ 3\amp 6\amp -2\amp 4\amp 8\\ -1\amp -2\amp 2\amp 1\amp -1 \end{bmatrix} \amp \xrightarrow[\hspace{35pt}]{r_2-3r_1}\amp \begin{bmatrix}1\amp 2\amp -1\amp -1\amp 3\\ 0\amp 0\amp 1\amp 7\amp -1\\ -1\amp -2\amp 2\amp 1\amp -1 \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp \xrightarrow[\hspace{35pt}]{r_3+r_1 }\amp \begin{bmatrix}1\amp 2\amp -1\amp -1\amp 3\\ 0\amp 0\amp 1\amp 7\amp -1\\ 0\amp 0\amp 1\amp 0\amp 2 \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp \xrightarrow[\hspace{35pt}]{r_3-r_2 }\amp \begin{bmatrix}1\amp 2\amp -1\amp -1\amp 3\\ 0\amp 0\amp 1\amp 7\amp -1\\ 0\amp 0\amp 0\amp -7\amp 3 \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp \xrightarrow[\hspace{35pt}]{-\frac{1}{7}r_3}\amp \begin{bmatrix}1\amp 2\amp -1\amp -1\amp 3\\ 0\amp 0\amp 1\amp 7\amp -1\\ 0\amp 0\amp 0\amp 1\amp -\frac{3}{7} \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
    </md>.
    The corresponding equivalent system is
    <md>
      L'\colon \
      \begin{linsys}{4} x_1\amp +\amp 2x_2\amp -\amp x_3\amp-\amp x_4\amp=\amp 3\\  \amp \amp \amp \amp x_3\amp + \amp 7x_4\amp =\amp -1\\ \amp \amp \amp \amp \amp  \amp x_4\amp = \amp -\frac{3}{7} \end{linsys}
    </md>.
    To get a system in reduced row echelon form, we continue row reducing the row echelon matrix above using Gauss-Jordan elimination, beginning by eliminating the nonzero entries above the rightmost leading one: 
    <md>
      <mrow> \begin{bmatrix}1\amp 2\amp -1\amp -1\amp 3\\ 0\amp 0\amp 1\amp 7\amp -1\\ 0\amp 0\amp 0\amp 1\amp -\frac{3}{7} \end{bmatrix} \amp \xrightarrow[\hspace{35pt}]{r_2-7r_3} \amp \begin{bmatrix}1\amp 2\amp -1\amp -1\amp 3\\ 0\amp 0\amp 1\amp 0\amp 2 \\ 0\amp 0\amp 0\amp 1\amp -\frac{3}{7} \end{bmatrix} </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp \xrightarrow[\hspace{35pt}]{r_1+r_3} \amp \begin{bmatrix}1\amp 2\amp -1\amp 0\amp \frac{18}{7}\\ 0\amp 0\amp 1\amp 0\amp 2 \\ 0\amp 0\amp 0\amp 1\amp -\frac{3}{7} \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
      <mrow> \amp \xrightarrow[\hspace{35pt}]{r_1+r_2} \amp \begin{bmatrix}1\amp 2\amp 0\amp 0\amp \frac{32}{7}\\ 0\amp 0\amp 1\amp 0\amp 2 \\ 0\amp 0\amp 0\amp 1\amp -\frac{3}{7} \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
    </md>.
    The corresponding equivalent system is
    <md>
      L''\colon \
      \begin{linsys}{4} x_1\amp +\amp 2x_2\amp \amp \amp \amp \amp =\amp \frac{32}{7}\\  \amp \amp \amp \amp x_3\amp \amp \amp =\amp 2\\ \amp \amp \amp \amp \amp  \amp x_4\amp = \amp -\frac{3}{7} \end{linsys}
    </md>.
  </p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      <md>
        \begin{linsys}{4} x_1\amp +\amp x_2\amp -\amp x_3\amp +\amp x_4\amp =\amp 1\\ -2x_1\amp -\amp 2x_2\amp +\amp 2x_3\amp -\amp 2x_4\amp =\amp -2\\ x_1\amp +\amp x_2\amp +\amp x_3\amp +\amp 2x_4\amp =\amp 3 \end{linsys}
      </md>
    </p>
  </statement>

</exercise>
<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      <md>
       <mrow>y_1-7y_2+6y_4 \amp = 5\\
        y_3-2y_4\amp = -3 \\
        -y_1+7y_2-4y_3+2y_4\amp =7
       </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </statement>

</exercise>
<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      <md>
        \begin{linsys}{3} 2x_1 \amp +\amp  2x_2  \amp +\amp 2x_3\amp =\amp 0\\ -2x_1 \amp +\amp  5x_2 \amp +\amp 2x_3\amp =\amp 1\\ 8x_1 \amp +\amp   x_2   \amp +\amp 4x_3\amp =\amp -1 \end{linsys}
      </md>
    </p>
  </statement>

</exercise>
<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      <md>
        \begin{linsys}{3} \amp \amp -2b  \amp +\amp 3c\amp =\amp 1\\ 3a \amp +\amp  6b \amp -\amp 3c\amp =\amp -2\\ 6a \amp +\amp   6b   \amp +\amp 3c\amp =\amp 5 \end{linsys}
      </md>

    </p>
  </statement>

</exercise>
<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      <md>
        \begin{linsys}{5} \amp  \amp  \amp \amp  T_3\amp +\amp T_4\amp +\amp T_5 \amp =\amp 0\\ -T_1\amp -\amp T_2 \amp +\amp 2T_3 \amp -\amp 3T_4\amp +\amp T_5 \amp =\amp 0\\ T_1\amp + \amp T_2 \amp -\amp 2T_3 \amp \amp \amp -\amp T_5 \amp =\amp 0\\ 2T_1\amp + \amp 2T_2 \amp -\amp T_3 \amp \amp \amp +\amp T_5 \amp =\amp 0 \end{linsys}
      </md>

    </p>
  </statement>

</exercise>
</exercisegroup>
<exercise>
  <title>True or false</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      If true, provide a proof; if false, provide an explicit counterexample. 
      <ol marker="(a)">
        <li>
          <p>
            The augmented matrix associate to a system of <m>m</m> linear equations in <m>n</m> unknowns is <m>n\times m</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The augmented matrix associate to a system of <m>m</m> linear equations in <m>n</m> unknowns is <m>m\times n</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Given a matrix <m>A</m>, there is a matrix <m>B</m> in row echelon form that is row equivalent to <m>A</m>. 
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Given a matrix <m>A</m>, there is a <em>unique</em> matrix <m>B</m> in row echelon form that is row equivalent to <m>A</m>. 
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            An <m>m\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> is row echelon form can have at most <m>m</m> leading ones. 
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            An <m>m\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> is row echelon form can have at most <m>n</m> leading ones. 
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            An <m>m\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> is row echelon form can have at most <m>\min\{m,n\}</m> leading ones. 
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If every column of a matrix in row echelon form contains a leading one, then all entries that are not leading ones are equal to zero. 
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If every column of a matrix in reduced row echelon form contains a leading one, then all entries that are not leading ones are equal to zero. 
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>
</subexercises>
</exercises>
